----
== Introducing YAMLSchema

* YAML has always needed a schema language

* JSON Schema requires a lot of work
  * for little gain

  YAMLSchema vs JSON Schema
$ vim -O person.ysd.yaml person.schema.json

* YAMLSchema compiles to JSON Schema (and back!)
* ysd is the YAMLSchema CLI
  * Converts back and forth
  * Tests faithful roundtrip

----
== Using YAMLSchema

> in-1 ysd
$ ysd person.ysd.yaml
$ ysd person.schema.json

  Check roundtrip (or diff):
$ ysd -R person.ysd.yaml

---- skip
  Compile ysd to ysdc form:
$ ysd -t ysdc person.ysd.yaml
$ vim -O person.ysd.yaml person.ysdc.yaml

----
== YAMLSchema is Written in YAMLScript!

* YSD works in the browser
  * Same YS code for CLI (compiled to Native)
  * and the browser (compiled to Wasm)

* YSD YS code written by GPT-5.6 using the YS skill
  * Biggest YS program I know of (5500+ loc)
  * https://github.com/yaml/yamlschema/blob/main/lib/ysd/core.ys

* https://yamlschema.org

* JSON Schema is a weak language
  * But fairly ubiquitous
* YAMLSchema will soon add YAMLScript support to schemas
  * Define your own constraint functions in YAML!

---- skip
* Not just about data validation
  * Also about perfect code generation

----
== Introducing YAMLStar

* A YAML framework for 32 programming languages (so far!)
  * https://yamlstar.org
* YAMLStar is YAMLScript without "Script"!
* Has a CLI called "yaml". Very similar to "go-yaml" CLI.
> in-1 yaml
$ <<<'a: [ok, 1, 2.3, true, null]' yaml -J
$ <<<'a: [ok, 1, 2.3, true, null]' go-yaml -J

* YAMLStar works the same in every language
  * Same API, same features, same bugs, same fixes
* YAMLStar has a Plugin System!
  * Same plugins for all languages
  * Most plugins are shared with go-yaml

* YAMLStar is how we get to YAML 1.3 (or 2.0)!
  * Working implementations in every language *before* release!
  * Plugins let us experiment with YAML 1.2…
    before deciding what goes into the next YAML spec!

----
== Introducing YAML Family Plugins

* The rest of this talk is about the YAML Family Plugins
  * Some are implemented already, some are being planned

* Plugins can be (and are!) written in Go, Glojure & YAMLScript
  * Compiled to Go libraries for go-yaml and Go programs
  * Compiled to shared libraries for YAMLStar

* A Plugin is like a complex Option
  * Can be highly configurable
  * Deep reach into go-yaml & YAMLStar subsystems

---- skip
* Parts go-yaml legacy code moved into default plugins
  * Made replaceable and configurable

* Each type of plugin has a named API
  * Third parties CANNOT make new plugin APIs
  * Third parties CAN publish new plugin API implementations
  * Users can choose and configure implementations

----
== The YAML Family Plugin APIs

* YAML-Parser - replace the entire parser stage
  * Standardize on YAML event structs
  * Performance, correctness, capabilities
  * Many YAML parsers exist and can be made pluggable

* YAML-Rules - custom loading and dumping for YAML
  * Make your own YAML DSLs
  * Custom tag resolution, construction and roundtripping

* Comment-Model - loading, dumping, roundtripping comments
  * YAML was designed to ignore comments. Sanity.
  * Several YAML frameworks support loading comments
  * All work differently. Plugins let you choose.

* Tab-Indent - use tabs instead of space for YAML indentation
  * Biggest complaint about YAML overall
  * YAML team tried hard for tabs in early years
  * Now available to try using plugins

----
== The YAML Family Plugin APIs

* YAML-Schema - apply constraints at load time
  * Use schema language of your choice
  * Works with YAMLSchema of course

* YAML-Format - custom, per node formatting
  * Full control over how each node or node type gets formatted

* Function-Tags - !shuffle shuffle a sequence
  * Map tags to custom functions / libraries
  * Works best with YAML-Rules (implicit tagging)
  * Create your own mini-YAMLScript

* Meta-Tag - top level tag declaring full intent
  * Associate a YAML to its Rules/Schema/Formatting etc
  * A tag that declares how YAML is expected to be processed
  * URLs work well - !kubernetes.io/yaml/service/v1
  * Optional. Application determines how tag is used

----
== The YAML Family Plugin APIs

* Error-Format - customize YAML error formatting
  * Allows YAML to change without breaking user expectations

* I18N-Translate - YAML keys in any language
  * Translates to canonical language
  * Canonical langauge matches schema

* JSON-Comments - support JSONC C-style comments in YAML
  * Support loading YAML with /* comment */ and // comment
  * Usefulness is Unknown!

---- skip
* Multi-Tag - support for multiple tags on node
  * YAML supports one tag per node
  * Multiple could be useful
  * !foo;bar or !foo !bar

* Context-Data - provide deterministic context to Loaders
  * Environment variables
  * External data - file, web, database

