== YAML Plugin Demos

* Plugins are being written for go-yaml & YAMLStar
* Mostly for both at once
* Written in Go, Glojure and YS
* Published as Go code repos w/ shared lib releases

----
== The YAML Family Plugin APIs

Let's Demo some real YAML Plugins!

----
== A Demo of the "JSON-Comments" Plugin

* YAML uses # for comments.
* JSONC added C-style comments
  * `//...` or `/*...*/`

* Two users requested that support be added to YAML:
  * https://github.com/yaml/yamlstar/issues/7

* Now available as a Plugin!
  * For YAMLStar and go-yaml

----
== Using JSON-Comments Plugin in Python Code

$ vim json-comments.yaml json-comments.py -O

$ pip install yamlstar

$ python json-comments.py

----
== Using JSON-Comments Plugin from the CLIs

  YAMLStar ('yaml' CLI):
> in-1 yamlstar
$ yaml --plugin=json-comments json-comments.yaml -J

  go-yaml CLI:
> make -C go-yaml cli PLUGIN=json-comments
$ go-yaml -J json-comments.yaml

----
== Tab-Indent Plugin

* YAML only allows spaces for indentation
  * We Really Tried to Support Tabs in 2003!

* The tab-indent plugin allows tab indentation

* Mixing spaces and tabs (like Python) is provably ambiguous

----
== Demo of the Tab-Indent Plugin

  A tab-indented YAML file:
$ vim tab-indent.yaml

  Standard YAML rejects tabs:
$ go-yaml -J tab-indent.yaml

  Build go-yaml with tab-indent:
> make -C go-yaml cli PLUGIN=tab-indent
$ go-yaml --plugin=tab-indent -J tab-indent.yaml

  The same plugin in YAMLStar:
$ yaml --plugin=tab-indent -Y tab-indent.yaml

----
== YAML-Parser Plugin: "reference"

* Parsing is the first stage in the YAML Load stack

* Different parsers make different tradeoffs
  * Compatibility, correctness, performance

* Plugins let applications choose the parser
  * Without changing the rest of the Load stack

----
== Demo of the YAML-Parser Plugin: "reference"

* https://play.yaml.com/?show=gdhflLnJPpxHUrsS#OiBmb28=

  The native go-yaml parser:
$ <<<': foo' go-yaml -Y

  Build go-yaml with the YAML reference parser:
> make -C go-yaml cli PLUGIN=parser=reference
$ <<<': foo' go-yaml -Y

  YAMLStar ships with both parsers:
$ <<<': foo' yaml --plugin=parser=go-yaml -Y
$ <<<': foo' yaml --plugin=parser=reference -Y

  Same load stack, different parser
* The reference parser follows the YAML 1.2 grammar
* go-yaml remains the native compatibility choice

----
== Demo of a YAML-Parser Plugin: "toml"

* What if we parsed TOML into YAML events?!

  YAMLStar:
$ vim settings.toml
* Show Parser Events:
$ yaml --plugin=parser=toml settings.toml -e
* Show result as YAML
$ yaml --plugin=parser=toml settings.toml -y

  go-yaml:
> make -C go-yaml cli PLUGIN=parser=toml
$ go-yaml -y settings.toml

----
== Alias-Data Plugin

* Ordinary aliases resolve anchors in the current document
* Alias-Data adds values from:
  * Inline configuration, files and the environment
  * Anchors from previous documents in the stream
* It is opt-in: ordinary YAML behavior stays unchanged

----
== Alias-Data Plugin

  External values:
$ vim -O alias-data.yaml alias-data-config.yaml
$ SPEAKER=Ingy yaml --config=alias-data-config.yaml -y alias-data.yaml

  The same configuration in go-yaml:
$ make -C go-yaml cli
$ SPEAKER=Ingy go-yaml --config=alias-data-config.yaml -y alias-data.yaml

----
== Alias-Data Plugin

  An anchor in one document, an alias in the next:
$ vim alias-stream.yaml

  Ordinary YAML rejects the cross-document alias:
$ yaml -y alias-stream.yaml

  Alias-Data retains the anchor for the stream:
$ yaml --plugin=alias-data -y alias-stream.yaml
$ go-yaml --plugin=alias-data -y alias-stream.yaml

